Observed failedSteps includes index 0: seeking #nav-apply fails because ax assertion didn't pass: name hint maybe wrong? Let's look at observed after details: In after block for seek action, we have:

"nameHint":"Apply",
...
"role":null,
...

So the element found (#nav-apply) had role null (i.e., not recognized as a link). It seems the accessibility tree does not expose it as a link. Why? Possibly because the nav list items are hidden via CSS when viewport > breakpoint? But here it's desktop width (since menu button display none). However note: The header JS toggles class 'govuk-header__navigation--open' on menu button click. Initially, the navigation ul has attribute aria-label="Navigation menu", but also likely has attribute aria-hidden? Wait initial HTML shows:

<ul id="navigation" class="govuk-header__navigation" aria-label="Navigation menu">

No aria-hidden attribute. So why would role be null? Maybe due to some script that sets aria-hidden=true on load? Look at header.js: In init, they call syncState(this.$menu.classList.contains('govuk-header__navigation--open')). At start, the navigation ul does NOT have class 'govuk-header__navigation--open' (since it's not open). So isVisible = false. Then syncState sets:

this.$menuButton.classList.toggle('govuk-header__menu-button--open', isVisible) // false => remove class
this.$menuButton.setAttribute('aria-expanded', isVisible) // set to "false"
this.$menu.setAttribute('aria-hidden', !isVisible) // !false = true => set aria-hidden="true"

Thus the navigation ul gets aria-hidden="true" on init, making it invisible to assistive tech. That explains why role is null (since hidden). Indeed the observed after shows the element still present in DOM but aria-hidden true makes it not exposed.

But the requirement expects that the visible navigation links are exposed to AT as links. The bug is that on desktop, the navigation should be visible (not hidden) and accessible. The header component incorrectly hides the navigation on init for all widths; it should only hide when menu button is visible (i.e., mobile breakpoint). The CSS already hides the navigation via media query: at max-width:40.0525em, .govuk-header__navigation {display:none} and .govuk-header__navigation--open {display:block}. The JS should mirror that: only set aria-hidden when the navigation is visually hidden (i.e., when menu button is displayed). The current logic always hides on init regardless of breakpoint.

Thus fix: In Header.prototype.init, we should determine if the menu button is currently visible (i.e., not display:none) and set aria-hidden accordingly. Or we could simply not set aria-hidden on init; rely on CSS to hide/show and manage aria-hidden via toggle when menu button clicked. The existing syncState is used on menu button click to toggle aria-hidden based on isVisible (the class). So we should remove the initial syncState call, or set isVisible based on whether the menu button is hidden (i.e., if menu button's display is none). Simpler: In init, we can check if this.$menuButton.offsetParent !== null (i.e., not display:none) to see if it's visible. If menu button is visible (mobile), then navigation should be hidden initially (aria-hidden true). Else (desktop), navigation should be visible (aria-hidden false). Also we need to set aria-expanded accordingly.

Let's examine the code: Header.prototype.init currently:

if (!this.$module || !this.$menuButton || !this.$menu) { return; }

this.syncState(this.$menu.classList.contains('govuk-header__navigation--open'));
this.$menuButton.addEventListener('click', this.handleMenuButtonClick.bind(this));

We need to compute isVisible appropriately: For desktop, menu button is hidden (display:none), navigation should be visible (no --open class). So isVisible should be false? Wait syncState expects isVisible meaning menu is visible? In syncState: they set aria-expanded to isVisible, and aria-hidden to !isVisible. So if menu is visible (isVisible true) => aria-expanded true, aria-hidden false (menu shown). If menu not visible (isVisible false) => aria-expanded false, aria-hidden true (menu hidden). So for desktop, menu not visible => isVisible false => aria-expanded false, aria-hidden true? That would hide the menu, which is wrong. Actually on desktop, the menu is always visible (the navigation links are shown). The menu button is hidden, but the navigation is visible. The aria-expanded on the button indicates whether the menu is expanded (i.e., whether the navigation is shown via button). On desktop, the navigation is always shown, but the button is not used; we could keep aria-expanded false (since not expanded via button) but we need aria-hidden false on the navigation (so it's accessible). However syncState ties aria-hidden to !isVisible, meaning if isVisible false => aria-hidden true (hidden). That's opposite of what we need for desktop.

Thus the logic is flawed: They assume that the menu's visibility is tied to button's expanded state, which only applies in mobile where menu is hidden unless button expands it. For desktop, menu is always visible regardless of button state. So we need to adjust syncState to consider a flag indicating whether we are in "collapsible" mode (i.e., button visible). If button is hidden (desktop), we should force menu visible and aria-hidden false, and aria-expanded can stay false (or maybe not relevant). The button's aria-controls still points to navigation; we could leave aria-expanded false (since not expanded). That's fine.

Therefore edit: In Header.prototype.syncState, we need to know if the menu button is currently visible (i.e., not display:none). If button is visible (mobile), then use isVisible param to reflect menu open/closed state. If button is not visible (desktop), then we should ensure menu is visible (remove aria-hidden) and maybe set aria-expanded false.

Simplify: Instead of changing syncState signature, we can change init to not call syncState at all, and rely on CSS and the click handler to set aria-hidden correctly when button clicked. But we need initial state correct: On desktop, menu should be visible and aria-hidden false. Since we won't call syncState, the navigation will have no aria-hidden attribute (default false). That's fine. On mobile, button is visible, navigation initially hidden (via CSS display:none). We also need aria-hidden true to hide from AT. Since we aren't setting it initially, it would lack aria-hidden, making it potentially visible to AT though visually hidden (bad). So we need to set aria-hidden true on mobile initially. Could detect button visibility and set accordingly.

Thus modify init: Determine if button is visible (offsetWidth > 0 or offsetHeight > 0 or getComputedStyle(...).display !== 'none'). If button is visible (mobile), set menu to hidden initially: this.$menu.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true'); this.$menuButton.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false'); else (desktop): ensure menu visible: this.$menu.removeAttribute('aria-hidden'); this.$menuButton.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false'); (button still false). Also we should not add class 'govuk-header__navigation--open' to menu initially (it shouldn't have it). The class is used for CSS to show menu when button clicked; on desktop we don't need that class.

Also need to adjust syncState to work correctly for both contexts: When button clicked (mobile), we toggle class and set attributes accordingly. That works fine because syncState uses isVisible from toggle result. For desktop, button is hidden (display:none) so click listener won't fire (button not visible). So syncState won't be called. Good.

Thus edit: In Header.prototype.init, replace the syncState call with logic to set initial aria-hidden based on button visibility.

